 ## Background
-Improved Perlin Noise (4D) is an extension of Ken Perlin's Improved Perlin Noise, which was introduced in 2002 to fix artifacts in the original 1985 Perlin Noise [S1, unofficial]. The 4D variant is commonly used in computer graphics for generating time-varying textures and in volume rendering for creating dynamic noise fields [S1, unofficial]. The GPU-optimized implementation using texture-based permutation and gradient tables was popularized in open-source projects such as "Marching Cubes On The GPU" [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Improved Perlin Noise (4D) is an extension of Ken Perlin's Improved Perlin Noise algorithm, implemented in GPU shaders for real-time procedural generation [S1, unofficial]. The 4D variant is commonly used in computer graphics for generating time-varying textures and in volume rendering for creating dynamic noise fields [S1, unofficial]. The GPU-optimized implementation using texture-based permutation and gradient tables is demonstrated in open-source projects such as "Marching Cubes On The GPU" [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
